1. What is the Oplog?
The oplog (short for "operations log") is a special collection in MongoDB that keeps a record of all changes made to the data in the database. It is stored in the local.oplog.rs collection on each member of a replica set.

Think of the oplog as a journal where every change (insert, update, delete) is written down. This journal is used by the replica set members to stay in sync with each other.

2. Why is the Oplog Important?
The oplog is critical for replication in MongoDB. Here’s why:

When a change is made on the primary node, it is recorded in the oplog.

The secondary nodes read the oplog and apply the same changes to their own data.

This ensures that all members of the replica set have the same data.

3. How Does the Oplog Work?
Here’s how the oplog operates step by step:

Primary Node Writes to the Oplog:

When a write operation (e.g., insert, update, delete) occurs on the primary, it is recorded in the oplog.

Each entry in the oplog is idempotent, meaning it can be applied multiple times without causing issues.

Secondary Nodes Read the Oplog:

The secondary nodes continuously read the oplog from the primary.

They apply the changes recorded in the oplog to their own data.

Asynchronous Replication:

The replication process is asynchronous, meaning the secondaries might lag slightly behind the primary.

Maintaining State:

By applying the changes from the oplog, the secondaries maintain the same state as the primary.

4. Where is the Oplog Stored?
The oplog is stored in the local.oplog.rs collection, which is part of the local database. This database is not replicated to other members.

5. How to View the Oplog?
You can view the contents of the oplog using the following command:

javascript
use local;
db.oplog.rs.find().pretty();
This will show you the operations recorded in the oplog.

6. How to Monitor the Oplog?
To check the size and status of the oplog, use the getReplicationInfo command:

javascript
db.getReplicationInfo();
This provides details like:

The size of the oplog.

The time range of operations stored in the oplog.

7. What Happens During Failovers?
If the primary node fails, one of the secondaries is elected as the new primary. The new primary continues to write to its own oplog, and the other members replicate from it.

8. How to Resize the Oplog?
If you need to change the size of the oplog, use the replSetResizeOplog command:

javascript
db.adminCommand({
  replSetResizeOplog: 1,
  size: 10240 // Size in MB
});
This allows you to dynamically resize the oplog without restarting the server.

9. Key Features of the Oplog
Capped Collection: The oplog is a capped collection, meaning it has a fixed size. When it reaches its size limit, the oldest entries are overwritten.

Idempotent Operations: Each operation in the oplog can be applied multiple times without causing issues.

Rolling Record: The oplog keeps a rolling record of changes, which is used for replication.

10. Use Cases of the Oplog
Replication: Ensures all replica set members have the same data.

Point-in-Time Recovery: The oplog can be used to restore the database to a specific point in time.

Change Streams: Applications can use the oplog to monitor real-time changes in the database.




Oplog Size
When you start a replica set member for the first time, MongoDB creates an oplog of a default size if you do not specify the oplog size.



The minimum oplog size is 990 MB. If 5% of free disk space or physical memory (whichever is applicable based on your storage engine) is less than 990 MB, the default oplog size is 990 MB.

The maximum default oplog size is 50 GB


2. How to Specify the Oplog Size Before Starting mongod
Before starting the mongod process, you can set the size of the oplog using the oplogSizeMB option. This defines the maximum size of the oplog in megabytes.

Example:
To set the oplog size to 500 MB:


mongod --oplogSizeMB 500


3. How to Resize the Oplog Dynamically
Once the replica set member is running, you can resize the oplog dynamically without restarting the mongod process. Use the replSetResizeOplog administrative command.

Example:
To resize the oplog to 1024 MB:

db.adminCommand({
  replSetResizeOplog: 1,
  size: 1024
});

..> 


4. Minimum Oplog Retention Period
The minimum oplog retention period ensures that oplog entries are preserved for a specified number of hours, even if the oplog reaches its maximum size. MongoDB removes oplog entries only if:

The oplog has reached its maximum size.

The oplog entry is older than the configured retention period.



How to Configure the Minimum Oplog Retention Period
You can configure the minimum oplog retention period in two ways:

Option 1: Configure at Startup
Add the storage.oplogMinRetentionHours setting to the mongod configuration file or use the --oplogMinRetentionHours command-line option.

Example (Configuration File):
yaml
storage:
  oplogMinRetentionHours: 24

Example (Command Line):
bash
mongod --oplogMinRetentionHours 24





Option 2: Configure on a Running mongod
Use the replSetResizeOplog command to set the minimum retention period dynamically.

Example:
To set the minimum retention period to 48 hours:

db.adminCommand({
  replSetResizeOplog: 1,
  minRetentionHours: 48
});

6. Important Notes
If you set the minimum retention period while mongod is running, it overrides any values set at startup.

To persist the changes through a server restart, update the configuration file or command-line option.

7. Monitoring the Oplog
To check the current oplog size and retention period, use:


db.getReplicationInfo();







