Replica Set Members :
------------------------------- >

.> The minimum recommended configuration for a replica set is a three member replica set with three data-bearing members: one primary and two secondary members. In some circumstances (such as you have a primary and a secondary but cost constraints prohibit adding another secondary), you may choose to include an arbiter. An arbiter participates in elections but does not hold data (i.e. does not provide data redundancy).

.> A replica set can have up to 50 members but only 7 voting members.


.> The primary is the only member in the replica set that receives write operations. MongoDB applies write operations on the primary and then records the operations on the primary's oplog. Secondary members replicate this log and apply the operations to their data sets.


.> All members of the replica set can accept read operations. However, by default, an application directs its read operations to the primary member. 
NOTE : WE CAN CHANGE THE 

.> A secondary maintains a copy of the primary's data set. To replicate data, a secondary applies operations from the primary's oplog to its own data set in an asynchronous process. [1] A replica set can have one or more secondaries.


.> You can configure a secondary member for a specific purpose. You can configure a secondary to:

Prevent it from becoming a primary in an election, which allows it to reside in a secondary data center or to serve as a cold standby. See Priority 0 Replica Set Members.

Prevent applications from reading from it, which allows it to run applications that require separation from normal traffic.


.> Keep a running "historical" snapshot for use in recovery from certain errors, such as unintentionally deleted databases.



Arbiter
In some circumstances (such as when you have a primary and a secondary, but cost constraints prohibit adding another secondary), you may choose to add an arbiter to your replica set. An arbiter participates in elections for primary but an arbiter does not have a copy of the data set and cannot become a primary.


.> An arbiter has exactly 1 election vote. By default an arbiter has priority 0.


.> Do not run an arbiter on systems that also host the primary or the secondary members of the replica set.


Warning :
============== >

Using a primary-secondary-arbiter (PSA) architecture for shards in a sharded cluster can cause a loss of availability if a data-bearing secondary is unavailable.

All members of the replica set can accept read operations. However, by default, an application directs its read operations to the primary member. 



@ Replica Set Secondary Members :
-------------------------------------------- >

..> To replicate data, a secondary applies operations from the primary's oplog to its own data set in an asynchronous process.  


..> Although clients cannot write data to secondaries, clients can read data from secondary members. 

 You can configure a secondary to:

Prevent it from becoming a primary in an election, which allows it to reside in a secondary data center or to serve as a cold standby. 
.> By using the Priority 0 Replica Set Members.

Prevent applications from reading from it, which allows it to run applications that require separation from normal traffic.

.> Hidden Replica Set Members.



Priority 0 Replica Set Members :
----------------------------------------- >

1) What is a Priority 0 Member?
A priority 0 member is a special type of secondary in a MongoDB replica set.

It cannot become the leader (primary) and cannot start elections to choose a new leader.

It can still:

Acknowledge writes (confirm that data has been saved).

Vote in elections (if it’s a voting member).



2. What is Write Concern?
Write concern is a setting that tells MongoDB how many members must confirm a write operation before it’s considered successful.

For example:

w: 1 means only the primary needs to confirm the write.

w: "majority" means most members (including voting members) must confirm the write.

A priority 0 member can acknowledge writes, but for w: "majority", it must be a voting member (i.e., it has votes > 0).



3. How Does a Priority 0 Member Work?
Other than the restrictions (can’t become primary or start elections), a priority 0 member works like a normal secondary:

It keeps a copy of the data.

It can handle read requests.

It can vote in elections (if it’s a voting member).




4. Why Use a Priority 0 Member?
Sometimes, you don’t want a certain member to become the leader (primary). For example:

If the member is in a distant data center with slower network speeds (higher latency), it’s not ideal for it to become the primary.

By setting its priority to 0, you ensure it won’t become the primary, but it can still:

Serve local read requests.

Help with voting in elections.




Priority 0 Members as Standbys
A standby member is a secondary that keeps a current copy of the data but doesn’t participate as actively as other members.

In some cases, you might set a standby member’s priority to 0 to ensure it doesn’t become the primary.

This is useful in setups with:

Different hardware (e.g., slower machines).

Geographically distributed members (e.g., members in different regions).


Failover Considerations
Failover happens when the primary becomes unavailable, and the replica set elects a new primary.

When using priority 0 members, ensure:

The main data center has enough voting members and members eligible to become primary.

This prevents situations where no member can become the primary.



Example Configuration
Here’s how to configure a member with priority 0:

javascript
rs.reconfig({
  _id: "myReplicaSet",
  members: [
    { _id: 0, host: "primary.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 1, host: "secondary1.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 2, host: "secondary2.example.com", priority: 0 } // Priority 0 member
  ]
});
In this setup:

The first two members can become primary.

The third member (priority 0) cannot become primary but can still vote and handle reads.



How Does a Priority 0 Member Acknowledge Writes?
A priority 0 member can acknowledge writes under certain conditions.

If the write concern is w: "majority", the priority 0 member must also be a voting member (it must have a votes > 0 setting in the replica set configuration). Without this, it won’t be counted as part of the majority.

What is a Voting Member?
A voting member is a member in the replica set that participates in elections and can vote for a new primary.

If votes: 0 is configured for a priority 0 member, it does not contribute to elections or acknowledging majority writes.

Example Configuration
Here’s an example of how this works:

Scenario:
You have a replica set with 3 members:

Member 1: Primary (priority 1, votes 1).

Member 2: Secondary (priority 1, votes 1).

Member 3: Secondary (priority 0, votes 0).

Behavior:
If you write data with w: "majority":

Only Member 1 and Member 2 (voting members) need to confirm the write.

Member 3 (non-voting) will NOT be counted as part of the majority.

If you change Member 3 to priority 0 but votes 1:

Now Member 3 can acknowledge writes with w: "majority" because it’s a voting member.

Why Does It Matter?
This setup is important for systems that:

Have priority 0 members in distant locations (e.g., another data center with high network delay). You might want them to acknowledge writes but never become primary.

Want to control which members are involved in elections and which ones are just backups.

Key Points to Remember:
A priority 0 member must be a voting member to count for w: "majority".

Non-voting members (votes: 0) are excluded from elections and majority acknowledgments.

Voting priority 0 members are useful for ensuring data redundancy and acknowledgments without promoting them to primary.






Hidden Replica Set Members : This will retriction from the clients reading  request to solve 
======================================== >

..> A hidden member maintains a copy of the primary's data set but is invisible to client applications. 

..> Hidden members are good for workloads with different usage patterns from the other members in the replica set.

..> Hidden members must always be priority 0 members and so cannot become primary.

..> The db.hello() method does not display hidden members. Hidden members, however, may vote in elections.

..> 



1. What are Hidden Members?
Hidden members are special secondary nodes in a MongoDB replica set that:

Keep a copy of the data from the primary node.

Are invisible to client applications unless you directly connect to them.

Do not receive traffic for read operations unless explicitly connected.


Hidden members are typically used for dedicated tasks like:

Reporting: Running analytics without affecting the primary.

Backups: Safely copying data without impacting the replica set.


2. How to Read from Hidden Members
You can only read from a hidden member if you directly connect to it. For example:

mongo --host hiddenMemberHost:port


..> If you connect to the replica set as a whole, hidden members won’t be included in the queries.



3. Delayed Members
Delayed members are secondary nodes configured to lag behind the primary by a specific amount of time (e.g., 10 minutes). They are useful for:

Disaster recovery: Keeping a copy of the data before recent changes.

Why Hide Delayed Members?
If delayed members are not hidden, applications might accidentally query outdated data.

Hidden delayed members prevent performance issues caused by lagging commit points during failovers.



Example Configuration:
To hide and make a delayed member non-voting:


rs.reconfig({
  members: [
    { _id: 0, host: "primary.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 1, host: "secondary.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 2, host: "delayed.example.com", priority: 0, hidden: true, votes: 0, slaveDelay: 600 } // 10-minute delay
  ]
});



4. Voting and Elections
Hidden members can still vote in elections to choose a new primary. However:

If you stop a voting hidden member, ensure the replica set still has enough voting members to maintain a majority. Otherwise, the primary will step down.

5. Backups with Hidden Members
Hidden members are ideal for backups because they don’t affect the replica set’s performance. Use the db.fsyncLock() command to safely lock the data files for copying.

Example:
To lock the data files for backup:


db.fsyncLock();
After copying the files, unlock them:


db.fsyncUnlock();


6. Write Concern:
------------------------------------- >

Hidden members can acknowledge write operations based on the write concern setting:

For w: <number> (e.g., w: 2), hidden members can acknowledge writes.

For w: "majority", hidden members must be voting members (votes > 0) to contribute to the majority acknowledgment.

Example Configuration:
To make a hidden member a voting member:

rs.reconfig({
  members: [
    { _id: 0, host: "primary.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 1, host: "secondary.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 2, host: "hidden.example.com", priority: 0, hidden: true, votes: 1 }
  ]
});



7. Performance Considerations :
--------------------------------------- >


..> If delayed members are not hidden and one or more nodes become unavailable:

..> The replica set might have to wait for the delayed member to acknowledge writes, causing performance issues.

..> Commit points will lag, leading to cache pressure and slower operations.



Delayed Replica Set Members :

..> Delayed members contain copies of a replica set's data set


Delayed members:

Must be priority 0 members. Set the priority to 0 to prevent a delayed member from becoming primary.

Must be hidden members. Always prevent applications from seeing and querying delayed members.

Do vote in elections for primary, if members[n].votes is set to 1. Ensuring that delayed members are non-voting by setting members[n].votes to 0 can help improve performance.





..> If your replica set contains delayed members ensure that the delayed members are hidden and non-voting.

..> Hiding delayed replica set members prevents applications from seeing and querying delayed data without a direct connection to that member. Making delayed replica set members non-voting means they will not count towards acknowledging write operations with write concern "majority".

..> 




Behavior
Delayed members copy and apply operations from the source oplog on a delay. When choosing the amount of delay, consider that the amount of delay:

must be equal to or greater than your expected maintenance window durations.

must be smaller than the capacity of the oplog. 




1. What is Sharding?
Sharding is a way to split large amounts of data across multiple servers (called shards) to improve performance and scalability. Each shard holds a portion of the data, and MongoDB manages how the data is distributed.




2. What are Delayed Members?
A delayed member is a secondary node in a replica set that applies changes (like inserts, updates, or deletes) after a delay. For example:

If the delay is set to 3600 seconds (1 hour), the delayed member will apply changes 1 hour after they happen on the primary.

Delayed members are useful for disaster recovery because they keep a copy of the data from an earlier point in time. If something goes wrong (like accidental data deletion), you can use the delayed member to recover the older data.


3. Why Delayed Members Have Limited Use in Sharded Clusters
In a sharded cluster, data is divided into chunks and moved between shards by a process called the balancer. The balancer ensures that data is evenly distributed across shards.

Problem with Delayed Members:

Delayed members replicate chunk migrations with a delay.

If a chunk is moved to another shard during the delay window, the delayed member’s data becomes outdated and cannot be used to recover the previous state of the sharded cluster.





rs.reconfig({
  members: [
    { _id: 0, host: "primary.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 1, host: "secondary1.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 2, host: "secondary2.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 3, host: "delayed.example.com", priority: 0, hidden: true, secondaryDelaySecs: 3600 }
  ]
});



A sharded cluster is a setup in MongoDB where large amounts of data are divided into smaller pieces called chunks. These chunks are distributed across multiple servers (called shards) to improve performance and scalability.


3. Why Delayed Members Have Limited Use in Sharded Clusters
In a sharded cluster, data chunks are moved between shards by a process called the balancer. The balancer ensures that data is evenly distributed across shards.

Problem with Delayed Members:
Delayed members replicate chunk migrations with a delay.

If a chunk is moved to another shard during the delay window, the delayed member’s data becomes outdated. This means:

The delayed member cannot be used to recover the previous state of the sharded cluster.

The delayed member’s data no longer matches the current state of the cluster.

4. Example Scenario
Initial Setup:
Shard A: Chunk 1 (Asia customers).

Shard B: Chunk 2 (Europe customers).

Shard C: Chunk 3 (America customers).

Delayed Member: Keeps a copy of the data with a 1-hour delay.

What Happens During a Chunk Migration:
The balancer moves Chunk 2 (Europe customers) from Shard B to Shard C.

The delayed member replicates this migration 1 hour later.

Problem:
During the 1-hour delay, the delayed member still shows Chunk 2 on Shard B, even though it has already been moved to Shard C.

If you try to recover the cluster using the delayed member, the data will be incorrect because it doesn’t reflect the chunk migration.

5. Configuration of a Delayed Member
To configure a delayed member, you set the following parameters:

priority: 0: Prevents the delayed member from becoming primary.

hidden: true: Hides the delayed member from applications.

secondaryDelaySecs: Sets the delay in seconds.

Example Configuration:
javascript
rs.reconfig({
  members: [
    { _id: 0, host: "primary.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 1, host: "secondary1.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 2, host: "secondary2.example.com", priority: 1 },
    { _id: 3, host: "delayed.example.com", priority: 0, hidden: true, secondaryDelaySecs: 3600 }
  ]
});






